  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "intervals-applied",
      "title": "Intervals — Applied",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** theory | **Tier:** applied | **Difficulty:** 4/10\n**Prerequisites:** Intervals — Introduction\n**Tags:** intervals, harmony, compound"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Invert intervals, spell them in any key, and recognise compound intervals (beyond the octave) in repertoire."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "inversion",
      "title": "Inversion",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Invert an interval by moving the lower note up an octave (or lower the upper). **Rule:** simple intervals sum to 9 (third ↔ sixth). Major becomes minor and vice versa; perfect stays perfect."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "compound-intervals",
      "title": "Compound intervals",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Ninth = octave + second. Eleventh = octave + fourth. Used in jazz extensions and some folk ornaments — label the simple interval first, then add octave."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "harmonic-use",
      "title": "Harmonic use",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Major and minor thirds build triads (next chord tier). Perfect fifths stabilise; tritones create tension resolved in cadences."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "entities": [],
  "entity_order": [],
  "playlist": [],
  "tunes": [],
  "key_points": [],
  "reading_list": [],
  "quiz_markdown": "### Q1. Inversion of a major third is:\n\n- A) Minor sixth\n- B) Major sixth\n- C) Perfect fifth\n- D) Minor third\n\n**Answer:** A\n\n### Q2. Simple intervals invert to sum to nine.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q3. Compound ninth is an octave plus a second.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q4. Major third plus minor third stack to major triad.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q5. Tritone is augmented fourth or diminished fifth.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q6. Spell M3 above G: B.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q7. Perfect intervals invert to perfect.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q8. Applied intervals support chord spelling.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q9. Compound intervals exceed an octave.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q10. Minor sixth inverts to major third.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q11. Interval fluency speeds transposition.\n\n**Answer:** True\n\n### Q12. Harmonic series relates to perfect fifth and octave.\n\n**Answer:** True",
